{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And behold, your eyes see, and the eyes of my brother Binyamin.</b> He equated Binyamin to the rest of his brothers, to teach that his love for them was equal in his eyes to his love for Binyamin, who had no part in his sale. <b>For it is my mouth that speaks to you.</b> In the holy tongue. And Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote that this is not a proof, for a single man in Egypt might speak in the holy tongue, and many in Egypt know it, even the ruler, for it is the way of kings and rulers to know languages. And the conclusive proof is in his mentioning his name and the matter of the sale: ״אני יוסף אחיכם אשר מכרתם אותי״. But the intent of the verse is to say: behold, your eyes see and the eyes of my brother Binyamin — that I, the ruler and master over all Egypt, am telling you by my own mouth that I am your brother, and I command you to bring down my father to sustain him." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״כי פי המדבר אליכם״ — ״כפי כן לבי״ (just so is my heart). And לב (heart) is not mentioned in the verse, but the Rabbis, of blessed memory, darshened it thus, because he could have said ״כי אני המדבר אליכם״ (for it is I who speak to you); and the reason he mentioned ״כי פי״ (for my mouth) is that speech placed in the mouth is the vessel of the heart, to bring forth what is within it — and from this they darshened ״כפי כן לבי״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:23.748448535Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}